---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('privy')!;
const b = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-brevo';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/privy-vs-brevo');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/privy-vs-brevo');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Privy or Brevo for cart recovery?', answer: 'Start with the bottleneck. Evaluate Privy when onsite signup capture and ecommerce email are the main job. Evaluate Brevo when you want cart events to feed an existing Brevo automation setup. Prove identified-cart entry, cart updates and purchase exits in the installed integration before choosing.' },
  { question: 'What should a Brevo integration trial prove?', answer: 'Trace cart_updated, cart_deleted and order_completed from Shopify to the intended contact and cart. Check that a cart update refreshes the message data, a deleted cart cannot send a stale reminder, and a purchase stops pending recovery.' },
  { question: 'Can Privy capture signups while Brevo sends?', answer: 'Treat this as a proposed integration design, not a guaranteed connection. Establish how consent, signup source, coupon promise and suppression changes move between systems. Assign one sender to each welcome or recovery message and test failure handling before enabling both.' },
  { question: 'How should discounts be compared?', answer: 'Subtract the discount from revenue while holding product cost constant. In a hypothetical $672 cart with 53% product gross margin, product cost is $315.84. A 20% discount leaves $537.60 revenue and $221.76 product gross profit, before fees, delivery costs and messaging costs.' },
  { question: 'Does attributed revenue show which tool is better?', answer: 'No. Attribution shows orders credited under a reporting rule. Compare the same eligible audience and accounting period, remove cancellations and returns, and use a suitably designed holdout when measuring incremental profit.' },
  { question: 'Should Sequenzy be added to either setup?', answer: 'Only evaluate another platform for a specific unmet requirement. Adding a sender does not establish shared suppression or coordinated queues. Document the needed integration and message ownership before accepting the extra cost and operating work.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide whether your next improvement belongs in onsite signup capture or in the cart-event automation behind your reminders. The useful comparison is the complete path from a known shopper to a current, eligible cart.</p>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the part of recovery that is missing</h2>
        <p>A store with traffic but few reachable shoppers has a different problem from a store with identified carts that never enter its email flow. For the first store, the trial should examine form targeting, the signup offer and how the promised message arrives. For the second, it should examine contact identification, event payloads and automation entry. Buying a different email editor will not repair an unidentified cart.</p>
        <p><a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy describes popups, email, SMS and automation</a> as parts of its ecommerce platform. That makes it a candidate when the owner wants to manage list growth and ecommerce messaging together. This product overview establishes scope, not a measured conversion advantage or a guarantee for a particular theme.</p>
        <p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360021501460-Which-Brevo-plugins-automatically-create-track-events">Brevo documents Shopify support for three cart and purchase events</a>. That is useful when assessing a store already using Brevo: there is a documented event vocabulary to trace rather than a reason to assume recovery is unavailable. Confirm the actual app version and installation path; a documentation table is not proof that your store is sending every required field.</p>
        <h2>Compare an operating workflow</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy evaluation</th><th>Brevo evaluation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Signup capture</td><td>Build the intended popup and inspect targeting and the offer shown to a returning subscriber.</td><td>Decide whether the existing capture source supplies enough identity and permission information for the flow.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recovery entry</td><td>Trace the installed recovery trigger to a known shopper and current cart.</td><td>Trace cart_updated into automation entry and distinguish a cart update from a completed purchase.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cart changes</td><td>Inspect the next queued message after a quantity or variant change.</td><td>Inspect event payloads and the values used by the email template after an update.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating cost</td><td>Quote the selected list-growth and messaging setup for your audience.</td><td>Quote the automation, contact and sending requirements of the actual Brevo setup.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance cases for the cart-event path</h2>
        <p>These are trial cases, not reported merchant results. Use test recipients and retain the event and queue records so a failure can be diagnosed. Run each case through the route you actually intend to use rather than mixing results from different plugins.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Anonymous visitor becomes known:</strong> add an item before submitting a signup form. Inspect whether the eventual contact is associated with the intended cart. If association is absent, record that limitation rather than counting every anonymous basket as recoverable.</li>
          <li><strong>Cart changes during a delay:</strong> change the selected variant and quantity after entry. Check which event updated the cart, whether the queued content uses the current items and whether a second entry created a duplicate reminder.</li>
          <li><strong>Cart is emptied:</strong> remove all items. For Brevo, trace the documented cart_deleted event; for either product, inspect the pending message and ensure it cannot advertise an empty or obsolete basket.</li>
          <li><strong>Purchase arrives before send:</strong> complete checkout while a reminder is pending. Trace the purchase event and verify that the intended recovery branch exits. Event arrival time and the configured waiting period are separate measurements.</li>
          <li><strong>Suppression changes after entry:</strong> unsubscribe the test contact while the reminder waits. Check the latest channel and purpose eligibility at dispatch. An address on a cart and permission to receive a marketing message are separate facts.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360002368860-Abandoned-cart-Send-an-abandoned-cart-email-to-recover-lost-sales">Brevo’s abandoned-cart guide</a> explains identified-contact entry and tracker-based recovery. Use that setup guidance alongside actual account logs. A missing identity, a missing event and an incorrect automation condition need different fixes; shortening the wait does not repair any of them.</p>
        <h2>If Privy captures and Brevo sends</h2>
        <p>A split arrangement needs a clear handoff. For a proposed welcome-offer flow, write down the signup source, email permission, offer shown, code issued and which system sends the promised email. Then determine the supported transfer method. Do not infer a supported direct integration from the fact that both services can store contacts.</p>
        <p>Test a transfer failure before relying on the arrangement: the form succeeds but the downstream contact update fails. The recovery procedure should preserve the original signup record and avoid issuing or sending the offer twice when processing resumes. A replayed contact update must not automatically start another welcome journey. Decide whether the existing subscriber should receive the offer at all before implementing the replay rule.</p>
        <p>Assign one owner for welcome delivery and one for cart reminders. Review Shopify’s existing recovery automation as well as the two apps. A contact-level unsubscribe sync cannot cancel a message already queued elsewhere unless the sending system actually checks or receives that change. If no supported cancellation or current eligibility check exists, simplify the sending arrangement before launch.</p>
        <h2>Keep the offer economics attached to the signup promise</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $672 cart at 53% product gross margin has $315.84 product cost and $356.16 product gross profit at full price. A 20% recovery discount reduces revenue by $134.40 to $537.60. With the same product cost, gross profit becomes $221.76, not $284.93. Fees, shipping subsidy, returns and messaging costs still need to be deducted.</p>
        <p>This matters particularly when an onsite welcome code precedes the reminder: check whether the original offer still applies before adding a second incentive. Inspect the actual checkout combination rules and exclusions. A payment or shipping question may call for assistance rather than another code. Choose discount eligibility from the order economics and offer policy, not a fixed percentage attached to a higher cart value.</p>
        <h2>Measure the improvement you are buying</h2>
        <p>For a capture-led Privy trial, count qualified new signups and fulfilled signup promises, then examine downstream purchases and unsubscribes. More submissions alone can conceal repeated existing subscribers or people attracted only by a code. For a Brevo automation trial, track eligible identified carts, successful entries, correct exits and messages delivered with current cart contents. Keep these operational results separate from sales credited by the platform.</p>
        <p>Use the same order exclusions and attribution window when comparing revenue. To estimate incremental profit, plan a randomized holdout with enough eligible shoppers for a meaningful result; size and duration depend on traffic and uncertainty. Deduct discounts and variable costs and do not add revenue claims from two platforms for the same order.</p>
        <h2>Choose a setup your team can maintain</h2>
        <p>Prefer evaluating Privy first when capture design and ecommerce messaging are the work your team wants in one place. Prefer evaluating the existing Brevo path first when the contact base is already there and the unresolved work is a traceable cart-event flow. Neither recommendation is a performance ranking: the acceptance cases determine whether the proposed setup meets the store’s needs.</p>
        <p>Compare current quotes at the same audience, send volume and required automation scope, including any capture tool, connector, SMS charges and maintenance time. There is no universal cheaper winner from a starting plan price. If considering Sequenzy as another option, identify the missing function and verify the required Shopify data path before adding another queue or subscription.</p>
        <p>At migration, keep a record of eligible pending carts, suppressions and already fulfilled offers. Stop new entries in the old sender, decide which pending messages to cancel, then enable the replacement with test recipients first. Retain a rollback owner and event records; avoid running two live recovery senders simply to compare dashboards.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Brevo FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
